@page "/components/heatmap"

<PageTitle>Heatmap — Unpoly.Blazor.Shadcn</PageTitle>

<h1 class="doc-h1">Heatmap</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">
    A calendar-shaped grid of intensity cells — one per value, oldest first. The buckets are cut
    from the series itself, so the ramp means "busiest day in this range" rather than a number you
    have to keep in step with the data. No JS.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Basic</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    Each cell holds its own count as text, so the calendar reads with no colour perception at all.
    Hover any cell for the same reading in a tooltip.
</p>
<Example Source="HeatmapExample.razor"><HeatmapExample /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Columns, cell size &amp; colour</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    <code>Columns</code> is days per column, <code>CellSize</code> the edge in pixels, and
    <code>ColorRamp</code> the Tailwind background utility the five steps scale — the alpha comes
    from it, so pass the bare token.
</p>
<Example Source="HeatmapCustom.razor"><HeatmapCustom /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Labels, column flow &amp; a shared scale</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    <code>Labels</code> names each cell, <code>Flow="columns"</code> fills a week per column,
    <code>Gap</code> sets the pixel spacing, <code>Max</code> fixes the ceiling so two grids share
    one scale, and <code>CellRadius</code> takes a literal Tailwind class.
</p>
<Example Source="HeatmapLabels.razor"><HeatmapLabels /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">When not to use it</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    A grid spends a whole column of pixels to say what one number says in a word, so a handful of
    values reads better as a plain list, a table or a <code>Chart</code>. The cells are
    deliberately date-less: a <code>role="list"</code> of counts, not a labelled calendar — pair it
    with real date headings if the day actually matters to the reader, and keep
    <code>GridPattern</code> for a lattice that carries no data at all.
</p>
